  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nWarm, breezy, with increasing clouds for the remainder of the day \nfollowed by rain and isolated thunderstorms as a cold front sags \nsoutheast. Excellent moisture return from the Gulf is evident on \nwater vapor, as anticyclonic flow off a southeast US ridge funnels \nmoisture poleward against a low swirling over the OK Panhandle. \nForecast soundings overnight depict an impressive warm-cloud layer \nof 12,000 ft, indicative of a moisture-rich atmosphere. Boundary-\nparallel flow through the column will limit the front's forward \nspeed. As a result, fairly high confidence in rainfall amounts \ntonight through late-morning Friday on either side of 1\". Some high- \nresolution ensembles have a few stripes of 2\" totals which is \ncertainly possible from any isoalted thunderstorms that bubble up. \nYet overall, with 10-day observed rainfall of less than 0.50\", the \ngeneral feeling is that the forecast area can handle this rainfall. \nWe can address any near-term flood concerns with Flood Advisories as \nneeded. \n\nCircling back to thunder for a moment, the best time for an isoalted \nthunderstorm is from about 4pm to 10pm EDT when a narrow ribbon of \ninstability is maximized; still shy of 1,000 j/kg. Tonight's rain \nlooks to be primary rain event for the next seven days. Low pressure \nthrough Ontario threatens to bring showers Sunday to central and \nnorthern Michigan, while a second system takes a similar path late \nnext week.\n\nSharply cooler behind the front resulting in highs Friday through \nthe weekend only in the 60s with Canadian high pressure moving in. \nAs mentioned here this morning, Tuesday morning looks absolutely \ncrisp with lows in the 30s. Advertised lows could be 2 to 3 degrees \non the cold side of available guidance (shy of the 25th percentile), \nbut within the realm of possibility. Fear not, as temperatures appear \npoised to rebound late in the week as upper-level troughing moves \neast.",
